CVPR-2025-P0603,CVPR-2025-A003781,Chunyi Li,shared,3,12,12,"Shanghai Jiao Tong University1 , Shanghai AI Lab2 , Moonshot AI3 , Nanyang Technological University4 Significant Gap! <Reference> <Distorted> <Reference> <Distorted> Human Visual System: <subjective perception> The image has a slightly higher saturation level. There is no change in the overall structure. The color of the tentacles has only been slightly changed. HVS Rating: . / ✅ The image is severely pixelated. The information of the clouds’ edge is completely lost. The detailed texture of the mountains is significantly changed. HVS Rating: . / ❎ Machine Visual System: <objective execution> The detected bounding box categories are different. The model mistakenly answers the object as a jellyfish instead of a spaceship, and judges the environment as amphibious rather than underwater. MVS Rating: . / ❎ The mask of the segmented mountains has hardly changed. The captions for both images are basically the same. The correct subject choice is still ‘steep peaks’, without misled by ‘grassland’. MVS Rating: . / ✅ Compare the similarity of images in terms of low-level attributes such as luminance and chrominance. Compare the consistency of downstream tasks such as segmentation and question answering. Figure . The significant gap between the well-explored Human Vision System (HVS) and the emerging Machine Vision System (MVS). Their different perception mechanisms leading to images that are subjectively satisfactory to humans not being applicable to the machines downstream tasks such as detection and question answering, vice versa.",-2,data/raw/cvpr/2025/first_pages/CVPR-2025-P0603.txt,https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2025/papers/Li_Image_Quality_Assessment_From_Human_to_Machine_Preference_CVPR_2025_paper.pdf
