CVPR-2025-P1550,CVPR-2025-A009662,Hanxiao Sun,shared,3,5,5,"Nanjing University Copy Flat Shading Constant Gaussian Interpolated Shading Spatially-varying Gaussian (Original) Interpolate Gaussian Normal (Ours) Gaussian Vertex Gaussian Vertex Normal Shading Point Shading Normal Material Attribute Novel View Synthesis Albedo & Normal Relighting Visibility & Indirect Shading Figure . SVG-IR introduces a new spatially-varying Gaussian representation inspired by replacing flat shading for interpolated shading in triangle rendering. Each spatially-varying Gaussian allows spatially-varying material and normal attributes by interpolating among Gaussian vertices defined in the tangent space of a Gaussian. Compared to the original Gaussian (i.e., Constant Gaussian) with constant attributes, SVG has a more powerful representation ability to produce high-quality NVS and relighting results. SVG-IR framework significantly improves rendering quality, outperforming state-of-the-art NeRF-based methods by . dB in peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) and surpassing existing Gaussian-based techniques by . dB in relighting tasks, all while maintaining a real-time rendering speed. The source code is available at",-14,data/raw/cvpr/2025/first_pages/CVPR-2025-P1550.txt,https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2025/papers/Sun_SVG-IR_Spatially-Varying_Gaussian_Splatting_for_Inverse_Rendering_CVPR_2025_paper.pdf
